func=jag::game::ServerConnection::ReadIncomingMessage	func_addr=0x000ff0c0	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=959	cu_index_from_entry=216	text=HOOK-CRITICAL: BUF_POS (conn+0x2d8) is RESET TO ZERO here, immediately after the payload read and\njust before PAYLOAD_READY (+0x35) is set. The fast-path re-entry at 0x000ff4d4 zeroes it too.\n\nSo on return from ReadIncomingMessage the cursor is 0, NOT the payload length. A capture hook that\nuses BUF_POS as the byte count reads zero bytes. Use RESOLVED_SIZE (conn+0x30) as the length.\n\nAlso note the payload is read to the START of the buffer (the Read dst is conn+0x2d0 itself, not\n+pos), so the packet body is bufData[0 .. resolvedSize).
func=jag::game::ServerConnection::ReadIncomingMessage	func_addr=0x000ff0c0	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=1008	cu_index_from_entry=228	text=FAILURE / INCOMPLETE-PACKET EXIT. Every short-read path lands here and writes BOTH halves of the\nout shared_ptr as NULL, then returns the out pointer in RAX.\n\nReached from: stream null or stream state !\e 2; GetAvailable()\e\e0 before the opcode byte;\nGetAvailable()<1 before the g1 size; GetAvailable()<2 before the g2 size; GetAvailable()<resolvedSize\nbefore the payload; and the unknown-opcode path at 0x000ff494.\nThe 0x000ff627 path (opcode valid, no ProtEntry) stores the global at 0x00ecd350/0x00ecd358, which\nis {0,0} - also an empty shared_ptr.\n\n\e> outMessage[1] \e\e NULL is a COMPLETE and reliable "no packet this call" discriminator. It is\nexactly what the caller FUN_000e9540 tests. A capture hook must gate on it or it will log torn\nbuffers as real packets.
func=jag::game::ServerConnection::ReadIncomingMessage	func_addr=0x000ff0c0	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=1316	cu_index_from_entry=309	text=HOOK-CRITICAL: the returned TcpConnectionMessage's OWN buffer receives only ONE byte - the prot\nopcode (msg+0x18 data, msg+0x20 pos incremented to 1). The PAYLOAD never enters the message; it\nstays in the ServerConnection's Packet at conn+0x2d0.\n\nConfirmed by the consumer FUN_000e9540, which hands the handler conn+0x2c0 (the ServerConnection's\nPacket), not the message's buffer, and takes only the prot from msg+0x38 and the size from msg+0x4.\n\nA hook that reads the returned message's buffer captures a single opcode byte and no body.
func=jag::game::NPC::NPC	func_addr=0x0039c610	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=0	cu_index_from_entry=0	text=NPC ctor. Identified by its sole caller FUN_001af2a0, which allocates from a pool of stride 0x11b0, places the NPC at poolSlot+0x20, and passes entityType \e 1 - the value Entity+0x10 must hold for the NPC branch. PathingEntity's own range ends at 0x1078; every field this ctor writes from param_1[0x20f] (+0x1078) upward is NPC-specific, and the object ends around +0x1178. +0x1078 holds the type pointer passed in by the caller.\nDO NOT import the Linux ONPC offsets. ID 0x1060 / TYPE_ID 0x1064 are inside PathingEntity's range on Windows (the base ctor writes 0x10001 at +0x1060). The NPC block appears shifted by roughly +0x18 relative to Linux, but that is arithmetic, not evidence - none of ID, TYPE_ID, CURRENT_HP, MAX_HP, HIDDEN_MENUOP_FLAGS or SHOW_AS_IMPORTANT has a confirmed Windows offset. Closing them needs a semantic read: the health-bar path for the HP pair, and the highlight_set_npc_show_as_important CS2 opcode handler (which checks entityType \e\e 1 then writes a bool) for the rest.
