func=jag::hal::RenderView::HandleMessage	func_addr=0x00728830	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=0	cu_index_from_entry=0	text=RenderView message handler - reached from jag::hal::JagRenderViewProc @0x00728770 via\nGWLP_USERDATA. Handles WM_CREATE (cursor load), WM_SIZE (client-rect cache + min/max state) and\nWM_SETCURSOR itself, then delegates EVERYTHING ELSE - all keyboard and mouse messages - to\n    FUN_0078a2d0(*(jag::input::Input**)(this + 0x10), hwnd, msg, wParam, lParam)\nfalling through to DefWindowProcA when that returns false.\nRenderView layout: +0x10 jag::input::Input* <-- THE INPUT OBJECT\n  +0x18/+0x19/+0x1a maximised/minimised/restore state bytes   +0x20 HWND\n  +0x28 pending cursor, +0x30 current cursor, +0x38 default arrow cursor, +0x40 cursor mutex\n  +0x90/+0x94/+0x98/+0x9c cached client rect (l/t/r/b)\nThe RenderView itself is the object the top-level frame holds at frame+0xe0 (see\njag::hal::JagWindowProc, which does SetFocus/MoveWindow on frame[0xe0]->[0x20]).\nNEXT STEP for the keyboard hooks: find the writer of RenderView+0x10 - it is created in the\nwindow thread FUN_00729ce0 - which gives the jag::input::Input constructor, hence its real vtable,\nhence slots +0x8/+0x10/+0x18 \e OnKeyDown/OnKeyUp/OnKeyChar.
func=jag::game::Entity::Entity	func_addr=0x00185140	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=0	cu_index_from_entry=0	text=jag::game::Entity::Entity(this, graphNode, worldRefSharedPtr, entityType, plane).\nLayout (identical to Linux): +0x0 vtable, +0x8 GraphNode*, +0x10 entityType, +0x11/+0x12 \e 6,6\n(one 16-bit 0x0606 store here; two byte stores on Linux), +0x14 pickType, +0x18/+0x20 screenX1/X2,\n+0x28 lineRadius, +0x30 screenCenterX, +0x38 pointRadius, +0x40 plane, +0x44 \e -1,\n+0x48/+0x50 shared_ptr<WorldReference> (atomic incref).\nEvidence: (1) the 6,6 initialiser at +0x11/+0x12 plus the -1 at +0x44 and the +0x48 shared_ptr\nincref are the exact Entity fingerprint; (2) writes precisely Entity's field range and nothing\nbeyond it; (3) called with the same 5-arg shape from the ProjectileEntity ctor (FUN_001a8d50),\nmirroring Linux ProjectileList::Add -> Entity::Entity, and from ~16 other derived-entity ctors.
func=jag::PacketHandlers::UpdateInvFull	func_addr=0x000fc010	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=0	cu_index_from_entry=0	text=jag::PacketHandlers::UpdateInvFull - handler+0x0 \e jag::Client*.\nWire: g2 inventoryId, g1 flags (bit0 \e secondary, bit1 \e per-slot vars follow), then\nCreateInventory(client[0x19988], id, flags&1) - a full snapshot always replaces the inventory,\nso unlike UpdateInvPartial there is no GetInventory lookup. Then g2 slotCount and exactly\nslotCount sequential entries (no per-entry slot index, which is the whole difference from\nUpdateInvPartial): count \e (g_protocolRevision < 0x3B5 ? g2 : g3), objId \e g1 with 0xFF -> g4,\nand if (flags&2) varCount \e g1 followed by varCount * (g2 varId, g4 value).\nTail: same dirty-inventory ring on client[0x19958] (+0xa8 / +0xb0) and the same sentinel return.\nEvidence: (1) the only caller of CreateInventory besides UpdateInvPartial; (2) byte-identical\nentry decode ladder, protocol-revision gate and var loop shared with UpdateInvPartial;\n(3) sequential-slot iteration bounded by a leading g2 count - the exact Full-vs-Partial contrast\npresent in the Linux pair - plus the identical dirty-ring tail and sentinel return.
