func=jag::MiniMenu::MiniMenu	func_addr=0x0012c2e0	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=0	cu_index_from_entry=0	text=jag::MiniMenu constructor. Builds the per-family action arrays, then calls the only two DoAction\nregistration functions in the binary:\n  jag::MiniMenu::RegisterComponentDoActions @0xe0690  -  7 actions (interface-component family)\n  jag::MiniMenu::RegisterDoActions          @0xe1b00  - 59 actions\n\e> 66 DoActions total for this build. NOTE: the inline list in RegisterDoActions' own comment is\nlabelled "61 entries" but actually enumerates 59; 59 is correct, 61 is a miscount.\n\nThe arrays built here are what fix the canonical op ordering, independent of the registration order:\n  loc array          c6aae0 c6ab40 c6aba0 c6ac00 c6ac60 c6acc0 -> ids 2 3 4 5 1001 1002 \e OBJECT_1..6\n  ground-item array  c6b210 c6b260 c6b2b0 c6b300 c6b350 c6b3a0 -> ids 18 19 20 21 22 1004 \e GROUND_ITEM_1..6\n  player array       c6b3f0..c6b6c0 then c6b710..c6b9e0       -> ids 44..53 then 2044..2053\nThis is what proves OBJECT_5/OBJECT_6 are ids 1001/1002 and NOT the alias ids 6/7, which merely\nreuse the same senders.
func=jag::opcode::highlight_set_npc_show_as_important	func_addr=0x001c1330	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=0	cu_index_from_entry=0	text=jag::opcode::highlight_set_npc_show_as_important - CS2 opcode handler.\nIdentified by its OWN string literal "highlight_set_npc_show_as_important" (0xb5f820, sole xref).\n\nGates on Entity+0x10 \e\e 1 (entityType NPC - the value NPC::NPC @0x39c610 passes), pops one int\noff the CS2 int stack and stores (v > 0) as a single bool.\n\n\e> ONPC.SHOW_AS_IMPORTANT \e 0x1120 on Windows. CERTAIN, two independent lines:\n   (1) the single bool this handler writes, on a pointer proven to be an NPC by the type gate;\n   (2) jag::game::NPC::NPC @0x39c610 zeroes exactly one byte at +0x1120 (param_1[0x224]).\n\nNOTE FOR FUTURE PORTS: the Linux value is 0x1100 and an earlier pass hypothesised a +0x18 block\nshift (which would have given 0x1118). The real shift is +0x20. The refusal to record the\narithmetic guess was correct - it would have been wrong by 8 bytes and read a neighbouring field.
func=jag::game::ClientError::ReportError	func_addr=0x003f7c40	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=0	cu_index_from_entry=0	text=jag::game::ClientError::ReportError(configProvider**, char* message, uint* stackTrace, flags).\nCrash/error telemetry funnel. Builds\n  "%s/nxtclienterror.w3>clientToken\e%s&timeStamp\e%llu&c\e%d&cs\e%d&nxt\e%s&u\e%s&cid\e%d&devbuild\e%d"\nfrom vtable getters on *param_1: +0x08 nxt version, +0x10 user, +0x18 cid, +0x20 clientToken,\n+0x30 port, +0x38 payload prefix, +0x40 cs, +0x48 c. Serialises the message plus, when\n(flags & 1) and stackTrace !\e null, a frame array - each frame rebased to a module-relative RVA\nvia GetModuleHandleA(NULL) - then urlencodes the blob into a "data\e" body and posts it.\n(flags & 4) routes to FUN_003f8230 first (dedupe / already-reported check) and suppresses the send.\nEvidence: (1) sole referencer of the telemetry format string @0x00b77ff0; (2) the vtable getters it\ncalls line up field-for-field with the query parameters that string names; (3) the frame-to-RVA\nrebasing plus "data\e" body is the crash-report shape of the Linux jag::game::ClientError::ReportError.\nThe engine hooks this to suppress telemetry.
