func=jag::ClientStream::Close	func_addr=0x0063c500	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=0	cu_index_from_entry=0	text=jag::ClientStream::Close(socket) - `this` here is the nested socket sub-object at ClientStream+0x8.\nClears the eastl::string at +0x20 (SSO marker byte at +0x27 restored to 0x17), zeroes the u16 at\n+0x48, sets the fd/SOCKET at +0x0 to -1 and calls ws2_32 closesocket (Ordinal_3).\nEvidence: identical field set and identical ordering to Linux jag::ClientStream::Close (which ends\nin close(fd) instead of closesocket), and it is the close used by Read/Write/Flush/GetAvailable.
func=jag::InterfaceManager::IfButtonXInner	func_addr=0x00170850	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=0	cu_index_from_entry=0	text=InterfaceComponent field evidence (componentRef[1] is the component): +0x8 ushort interfaceId, +0xa ushort componentId, +0xc ushort slotId, each 0xffff \e none. Packed as (interfaceId &lt;&lt; 16 | componentId) in the low dword of the CS2 hook arg with (slotId + 1) in the high dword. +0x68 \e hook table (hook id 0x27 looked up here). +0x130 \e eastl::string text (SSO marker at +0x147, heap size at +0x138); empty text selects the IF_BUTTON1..10 path, non-empty selects the with-text packet which also carries the byte at +0x148. CAUTION: +0xe is NOT a component field - the +0xe reads in FUN_00169e40 / FUN_0016fe20 are on a different struct (reached via component+0x100, or via a shared_ptr pointee) where +0x8 is an interface id and +0xe a slot index into a 0x18-stride array.
