func=jag::input::Input::OnKeyDownInner	func_addr=0x00033dc0	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=0	cu_index_from_entry=0	text=jag::input::Input::OnKeyDownInner(this, vkCode). Virtual, Input vtable @0x00b4ffd8 slot +0x08.\n`this` \e\e jag::Client + 0x18d80 (Input is embedded, as on Linux).\nLocks this+0x18, walks the named-handler rbtree this+0x70 -> anchor this+0x68 calling\nFUN_007896f0(node+0x38, vk) and stopping on the first handler that consumes, then falls through to\nthe global handler at this+0x08. Finally sets the pressed byte in the InputState key-down map.\nInputState key-down map (this is the structure Linux IsKeyDown probes):\n    anchor DAT_00c78400, root DAT_00c78410; node key \e int at node+0x20, pressed byte at node+0x24.\nMissing keys are inserted via FUN_0078e5e0 before the byte is set.\nThe vk 0x1CA3 sentinel is the console toggle: the dispatch pre-fires it when the incoming vk\nequals this+0xb8 (CONSOLE_KEY_VK, initialised to 0xDF by the Client ctor at client+0x18e38).\nThat path checks vk 0x12 (VK_MENU) in the same key-down map and sets the console-open flag at\nthis+0xc8, which OnKeyCharInner then uses to swallow character input.\nEvidence: (1) reached from Input vtable slot +0x08, which the WndProc dispatch FUN_0078a2d0 calls\nfor WM_KEYDOWN/WM_SYSKEYDOWN; (2) touches exactly the Input fields established from OnMouseMotion -\nmutex +0x18, rbtree +0x68/+0x70 with the value at node+0x38, globalHandler +0x08; (3) does NOT call\njag::input::Input::OnMouseMotion (0x0078bb90) - the discriminator that exposed the touch-handler\nclass twice in earlier tranches.
